---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaSection from '../../components/CtaSection.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { graph, breadcrumbs, faqSchema } from '../../lib/schema';
import { EMAIL, BOILERPLATE } from '../../data/site';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Should a new business wait until it's bigger before it gets a system?",
    a: 'No. Waiting means opening on paper, living with the pain of double entry for a few months, and migrating later under pressure once it already hurts. This shop skipped that phase entirely: the system was live before the first customer arrived, so there was never a paper process to migrate away from.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How fast can a system like this be ready for an opening day?',
    a: 'Fast enough to be running before day one, if the opening date is set early and the build is scoped against it. Here the system was in production about two weeks before the doors opened, which left time to work through it before a real customer touched it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it work on the phone at the counter, or only on a computer in the office?',
    a: 'Both, from the same system. It is built as a progressive web app, so the phone at the counter and the computer in the office run the same thing, with nothing to install.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens after opening day?',
    a: 'The system keeps recording every order, service, and invoice as the shop runs, and it shipped with documentation so the team is not dependent on us for day-to-day use. We stay involved for what comes next: reporting, and adjustments to the catalog and checklist once the shop has real weeks of data behind it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does the multiservice checklist actually prevent?',
    a: 'A skipped step on a job with more than one part, an oil change, a full inspection, that only gets noticed after the vehicle is already gone. The technician works down the same checklist every time instead of relying on memory, no matter how busy the shop is.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = graph(
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Case Studies', path: '/case-studies' },
    { name: 'Lube center', path: '/case-studies/lube-center' },
  ]),
  faqSchema(faqs)
);
---
<Base
  title="Lube Center: Born Digital From Day One | pocho.tech"
  description="A quick-lube shop's management system (service orders, checklist, invoicing, scheduling) went live before the first customer walked in. No paper phase, no migration later."
  path="/case-studies/lube-center"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="cs-header">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Lube center: open on a Monday, running on its system since the Friday before.</h1>
      <p class="client-line"><strong>A quick-lube shop.</strong> Opened by the owners of a brake-parts distributor we work with. One system: service orders, a catalog, a multiservice checklist, invoicing, and scheduling, live before the first customer arrived.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The decision</h2>
      <div class="decision-block">
        <p>Most new shops open on paper. A notebook at the counter, a folder for invoices, a whiteboard for the schedule, and a system that gets built later, once the paper is causing enough pain to justify the project.</p>
        <p>The owners chose the other order. Before the shop had a single customer, it had a system. The management system was live in production about two weeks before opening day, running with no orders to process yet, waiting for the doors to open.</p>
        <p>That is the decision in one sentence: build the system first, open the business on top of it, and skip the months of paper that every other new shop treats as normal.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What we built</h2>
      <p>One system, four jobs, no separate app for any of them.</p>
      <ol class="systems">
        <li><strong>Service orders</strong>: every vehicle gets one record from intake to close, so nobody has to ask what stage a car is at.</li>
        <li><strong>Service catalog</strong>: what the shop offers is defined once, the same way every time, instead of decided from memory at the counter.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiservice checklist</strong>: jobs with more than one step, an oil change, a full inspection, run down a checklist instead of memory, so a step doesn't get skipped because the shop is busy.</li>
        <li><strong>Invoicing and agenda</strong>: the order closes into an invoice, and the day's schedule runs off the same data, not a separate calendar.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="systems-note">It is a progressive web app. The phone at the counter and the computer in the office run the exact same system, with nothing to install.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <figure style="margin: 36px 0 8px; overflow-x: auto;">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 940 170" role="img" aria-label="Flow: system deployed two weeks before opening, doors open on Monday with the first order taken digitally, every service runs order, checklist, invoice" style="min-width: 760px; width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;">
          <defs>
            <marker id="arr-l" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="#6a6a6a" />
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <rect x="8" y="30" width="280" height="110" rx="10" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#e03a24" stroke-width="1.5" />
          <text x="28" y="60" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5" fill="#e03a24">TWO WEEKS BEFORE OPENING</text>
          <text x="28" y="86" font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#141414">System deployed</text>
          <text x="28" y="106" font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#141414">to production</text>
          <line x1="290" y1="85" x2="318" y2="85" stroke="#6a6a6a" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr-l)" />
          <rect x="323" y="30" width="280" height="110" rx="10" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#e6e6e2" />
          <text x="343" y="60" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5" fill="#6a6a6a">OPENING MONDAY</text>
          <text x="343" y="86" font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#141414">First customer,</text>
          <text x="343" y="106" font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#141414">first digital order. No paper.</text>
          <line x1="605" y1="85" x2="633" y2="85" stroke="#6a6a6a" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr-l)" />
          <rect x="638" y="30" width="295" height="110" rx="10" fill="#f6f6f4" stroke="#e6e6e2" />
          <text x="658" y="60" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5" fill="#6a6a6a">EVERY SERVICE</text>
          <text x="658" y="86" font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#141414">Order, then checklist,</text>
          <text x="658" y="106" font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#141414">then invoice. Never a skipped step.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #6a6a6a; margin-top: 10px;">One PWA: the counter phone and the office computer run the same system.</figcaption>
      </figure>

  <section class="cs-block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The opening</h2>
      <div class="decision-block">
        <p>The shop opened on a Monday. Its first customer's order went into the system directly, the same way every order since has. There was no clipboard to fill out first and no separate step to type it up later.</p>
        <p>That is the point of building the system before the business opens: there was never a paper process to reconcile against a digital one. No double-entry stage, no stretch of weeks where the counter runs on a notebook while someone catches the system up. The team's first order was already digital, and so was every one after it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The deployment</h2>
      <div class="decision-block">
        <p>Getting the system into production before opening day did not go smoothly on the first try. The first attempt used an automatic, fashionable deployment integration, the kind meant to push a project live with one click. It failed under the size of this project.</p>
        <p>What worked was the classic method: deploying by hand, from the command line. Slower to set up, and it doesn't have the same story to tell in a demo, but it worked, and the opening date wasn't moving.</p>
        <p class="systems-note">The lesson generalizes past this one shop: the boring tool that works beats the modern one that doesn't, on any project where the deadline is fixed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How we built it</h2>
      <div class="decision-block">
        <p>The build followed the method behind every system we ship: month one delivers the core that solves the most painful part of the process, not every feature the shop could eventually want. Here, that core was getting a vehicle from intake to invoice without losing track of it, with the checklist built in so multiservice jobs don't lose a step.</p>
        <p>It shipped with documentation, so running the shop day to day doesn't depend on reaching us. What comes after opening, reporting, adjustments to the catalog and checklist, is its own phase, scoped once the shop has real weeks of data behind it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq faqs={faqs} heading="Questions about opening this way" />

  <CtaSection
    heading="If you're opening a business and don't want a paper phase"
    body="Sixty minutes: you walk us through your opening timeline, and we tell you what's worth having live before day one and what it would cost."
    secondaryLabel={EMAIL}
    secondaryHref={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="boilerplate">{BOILERPLATE}</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .cs-header { padding-block: var(--space-section) clamp(36px, 5vw, 56px); }
  .client-line { color: var(--c-text-mute); max-width: 70ch; margin-top: 16px; }
  .client-line strong { color: var(--c-text); }

  .cs-block { padding-block: clamp(40px, 6vw, 64px); }

  .decision-block {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--c-border);
    padding-left: clamp(20px, 3vw, 32px);
    margin-left: 8px;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .decision-block p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .systems {
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: sys;
    margin: 28px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: 72ch;
  }
  .systems li {
    counter-increment: sys;
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 56px;
    margin-bottom: 24px;
  }
  .systems li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .systems li::before {
    content: counter(sys, decimal-leading-zero);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 3px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--c-text-dim);
  }
  .systems strong { color: var(--c-text); }
  .systems-note {
    margin-top: 28px;
    color: var(--c-text-mute);
    max-width: 70ch;
  }

  .boilerplate {
    color: var(--c-text-dim);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    font-style: italic;
    margin: 40px 0 0;
    max-width: 75ch;
  }
</style>
